 "observations": [
  "The page number «142» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 31», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. A speck in the left margin before the folios; not transcribed. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03 in smaller bold type, as on pp. 140-141 (JP2 0172-0173). The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the last title in two lines, «C 58r, O 115v,» and «N 27v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 141 (JP2 0173), with «Pagina numerorum (O)» alone over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium1)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium1)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Saturni examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio ipsius4) centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Saturni»; «Latitudo Saturni recedentis5)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L07), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L08, the units, in bold.",
  "L09-L38. Checked as on p. 138 (JP2 0170): the Sublimatio is 8s 4° 55′ less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 134° and 143°. At 143°, 8s 3° 2′ stands for 8s 3° 2½′. At 134°, 8s 2° 42′ answers to an Examinatio argumenti of 4° 26′ or 4° 27′, where the table has 4° 29′; its steps from 133° and to 135°, 2′ and 6′, stand against 4′ to 5′ on either side, so that the 29 is out of line in the column itself. No note of the apparatus bears on it.",
  "L09-L38. The Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.9″ in every row except at 136° and 143°: at 136°, 3° 29′ 24″ is 1′ more than 5° · sin 136° = 3° 28′ 23.9″, the 3° 28′ 24″ that p. 139 (JP2 0171) has at 44°; at 143°, 3° 0′ 43″ is 10″ more than 3° 0′ 32.7″, the 3° 0′ 33″ of 37° on p. 139. No note of the apparatus bears on either. At 122° and 144° the values agree with the computation, 4° 14′ 24″ and 2° 56′ 20″, where p. 139 has 4° 14′ 22″ at 58° and 2° 57′ 20″ at 36°.",
  "L09-L38, the other columns: the Examinatio centri for 180° − y is that of y on pp. 138-139 (JP2 0170-0171) in every row except at 122°, 124°, 126° and 135°, where it is 1′ larger, 7° 16′, 7° 5′, 6° 54′ and 6° 5′ against 7° 15′, 7° 4′, 6° 53′ and 6° 4′ at 58°, 56°, 54° and 45°. The Examinatio argumenti is 0.2′ to 2.2′ larger than the equation of the epicycle of radius 6 of p. 141 (JP2 0173), 3.8′ at 134°. The Statio prima increases from 3s 24° 51′ to 3s 25° 19′ by 1′ a degree (2′ from 122° to 123°), with 3s 25° 18′ at 147° and 148° and 3s 25° 19′ at 149° and 150°. The Latitudo prima is 1° 14′ to 134°, 1° 13′ to 142° and 1° 12′ from 143° on.",
  "Suter's examples. The Examinatio centri 4° 56′ 30″ for the centrum 144° 28′ (p. 53, JP2 0085, L16) lies halfway between 5° 0′ (144°, L32) and 4° 53′ (145°, L33), as for 144½°. The first station 115° 10′ for 139° (p. 56, JP2 0088, L01) is the 3s 25° 10′ of L27. The latitudo secunda 3° 48′ 27″ for 130° 24′ (p. 62, JP2 0094, L37) is the value interpolated between 3° 49′ 49″ (130°, L18) and 3° 46′ 25″ (131°, L19), 3° 48′ 27.4″.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the editor's readings agree with the rows around them where the variants depart: the Sublimatio 8s 2° 55′ and 8s 2° 58′ at 140° and 141° (notes b, C, against O's 3); the Examinatio argumenti 3° 25′ and 3° 19′ at 147° and 148° (notes c and d, C), each confirmed by the Sublimatio of its row, 8s 3° 13′ and 8s 3° 16′; the Examinatio centri 4° 18′ at 150° (note e), the value of 30°; the Latitudo prima from 121° to 150° (notes f-f), where C's 1° 24′ to 1° 22′ are 10′ larger, and 1° 14′ from 131° to 134° (notes g-g, C), against the 13 of O and N; the Latitudo secunda 2° 47′ 45″ at 146° (note h), 2° 47′ 45.5″ computed.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01 and N03-N04: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. N03-N04: «f-f) 14 . . . . 12]», the first and the last value of the range of the Latitudo prima, and «g-g)», each marking the first and the last place of a range."
 ],
